---
// Opening story for the Exemplars page: the reader decides first, then opens each outcome.
---
<section class="story-intro" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">A fictional scenario</p>
    <h2 id="story-heading">A decision on Monday morning</h2>
    <p class="story-scene">Maple Campus has no business analyst. A club organizer complains that their room was double-booked the day before a 60-person workshop, the third complaint like it since September. Maya Chen, who manages student services, has seen this before, and a vendor’s online booking system could start next month.</p>

    <div class="story-decision">
      <h3>You are Maya. What do you decide?</h3>
      <ul class="decision-options">
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="A" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>A</strong> Approve the new booking system now.</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="B" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>B</strong> Tell Facilities to answer every request within one working day.</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="C" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>C</strong> Ask the booking coordinator what went wrong, and fix whatever they name.</button></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Choose an option to see what happens. What would you need to know to be confident?</p>
    </div>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal" id="instinct-outcomes">
      <summary>What happened when Maya decided on instinct</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <ul class="story-outcomes">
          <li data-outcome="A" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>A:</strong> Six months later the budget is spent, but requests still arrive incomplete through email and chat, and one coordinator still approves every booking. Confirmations are a little faster; the complaints continue.</li>
          <li data-outcome="B" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>B:</strong> Facilities agrees, but replying faster does not fill in missing details or cover an absent approver. Within weeks the target slips.</li>
          <li data-outcome="C" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>C:</strong> Maya squeezes in a 15-minute chat with the coordinator between meetings. Under pressure, the coordinator names the latest frustration: organizers forget to give a head count. A reminder is added to the request email. Nobody else is asked, so the single approver and the hand-updated calendar stay hidden, and the real cause is never found.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>None of these choices was careless. Each drew on real experience, but Maya had no one whose job was to find out what was actually going on.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal">
      <summary>The same decision, with you as the business analyst</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <p>As a business analysis professional, you first ask: where do requests wait, what is missing when they arrive, and who approves when the coordinator is away? You run a <a href="#facilitation">workshop</a> with organizers, Facilities and the Accessibility Office, and you sample recent requests. Together they point to <a href="#causes">three likely causes</a>: incomplete requests from scattered channels, a single approver and a hand-updated calendar.</p>
        <p>Maya still decides, but now she approves a four-week pilot with <a href="#requirements">clear targets</a> you helped define and a plan for <a href="#risks">what could go wrong</a>. If it works, she has evidence to expand. If not, being wrong costs four weeks, not a system contract. The exemplars below show your work behind that decision.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<script>
  // A, B and C open the instinct outcomes and highlight the reader's choice.
  const outcomes = document.getElementById('instinct-outcomes') as HTMLDetailsElement | null;
  const buttons = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.decision-options button');
  buttons.forEach((button) => {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (!outcomes) return;
      const choice = button.dataset.choice;
      buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b === button)));
      outcomes.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-outcome]').forEach((item) => {
        item.classList.toggle('is-chosen', item.dataset.outcome === choice);
      });
      outcomes.open = true;
      const chosen = outcomes.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-outcome="${choice}"]`);
      const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
      chosen?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: reduceMotion ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'center' });
      chosen?.focus({ preventScroll: true });
    });
  });
</script>
